                <pb facs="0235" n="137" sameAs="frus:frus1930v02/pg137"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/10937: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1930-06-27T22:00:00+08:00">June 27, 1930—10
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received June 29—12:30 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>510. 1. Canton’s June 24, 1 p.m.<anchor n="48" xml:id="d127-f1"/> has been
                    repeated to the American Consuls at Foochow, Amoy and Swatow.</p>
                <p>2. Following from American Consul at Foochow: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“June 27, 10 a.m. Your June 26, 1 p.m.<anchor n="48" xml:id="d127-f2"/>
                            Ironsides apparently coming to join Lu Hsing-pang. Lu recently appointed
                            Commander <pb facs="0236" n="138" xml:id="pg138"/> of Eighth Army and
                            Governor of Fukien by the Northern Coalition. Thirty-six Americans still
                            in Lu territory unable to evacuate. River completely blocked.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <p>Repeated to the Commander in Chief.</p>
                <closer>For the Minister:<lb/><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                        >Perkins</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
